<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Malo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Malo is a walled port city on the Channel coast of Brittany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rance">Rance</a>. Named after the Welsh monk Malo, who founded a monastery at neighbouring Aleth in the 6th century, it became famous for its seafarers, privateers and explorers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a>. Its old town was largely destroyed in 1944 and rebuilt after the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-malo.jpg" alt="Saint-Malo" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saintmalo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
